=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: false
faulty_devices: none
diagnosis_text:
The network is operating correctly. All seven routers (router0 through router6)
have fully populated RIP routing tables and healthy FRR daemons (watchfrr,
zebra, ripd) running without interruption since lab startup. Router0 — the
first-hop gateway for PC4 and PC5 at 172.16.10.1 — carries a complete set of
RIP-learned and directly connected routes; there is no sign of a routing
daemon crash or table wipeout.

PC4 can reach every subnet in the topology except the mswitch2-attached
networks (172.18.50.0/24 and 172.18.51.0/24): pings to PC11 and PC13
(192.168.200.0/24, the router4/switch4/5/6 area) complete with 0% packet loss,
confirming that routing through router0→router5→router4 is fully functional.

The reason PC4 cannot reach PC15 (172.18.51.15) or laptop4 (172.18.50.14) is
a baseline iptables firewall on router6. Router6's startup script translates
the original Cisco "ip access-group 110 in" on Serial2/0 into four iptables
FORWARD rules on eth2 (the WAN uplink to router3): all TCP, UDP, and ICMP
arriving inbound on eth2 is dropped, with a single narrow exception for TCP
from 172.16.10.0/24 to 172.18.51.1 port 80. The drop counter on the catch-all
ICMP rule shows exactly 7 hits — matching only the diagnostic pings sent
during this investigation — confirming this ACL has been in place since lab
start and has not been injected as a fault. The ACL silently discards packets
before they reach the mswitch2 hosts, causing the timeout that the user
observes.

The user's belief that "routing died on our router" is incorrect. No routing
